# Code owners — automatic reviewer assignment.
#
# Ownership follows the project's decision rights: the lead owns the
# specifications, the correctness-critical statistical core, and anything that
# can widen the install closure. The rules below are the whole of it. Add
# individual handles as PIs and developers join; team handles work today and do
# not need updating when membership changes.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so order matters: general first, specific last.

# Default: the lead reviews anything not otherwise claimed.
*                       @sushovan4

# Specifications. Changing a published contract is a project-level decision,
# not a code review.
/rfcs/                  @sushovan4

# Correctness-critical statistical core. Every function here traces to an
# equation in Papers I-IV, and someone has to defend it to a referee.
# See CLAUDE.md, "AI assistance -- three tiers".
/src/akriti/core/       @sushovan4
/src/akriti/castle/     @sushovan4

# Licensing and supply chain. A change here can silently widen the default
# install closure -- the exact failure DEPENDENCIES.md exists to prevent.
/pyproject.toml         @sushovan4
/DEPENDENCIES.md        @sushovan4
/tools/                 @sushovan4
/SECURITY.md            @sushovan4

# Release and CI configuration.
/.github/               @sushovan4

# The interchange layer and packaging are the students' to own. Both of them
# review here, which is the arrangement in the onboarding document: they review
# each other's PRs, and Majhi reviews both.
/src/akriti/diagrams/   @akritihq/developers @sushovan4
/tests/                 @akritihq/developers @sushovan4
